WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.243
Picture a café app that gives you a free coffee for inviting a friend.

2
00:00:07.243 --> 00:00:11.396
Asha invited Chloe and Elena, and each of them invited a friend.

3
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:17.897
Now the company wants to thank Asha for her whole chain: everyone who joined through her, at any distance.

4
00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:25.428
The first query people write counts by levels: her friends, then her friends' friends, one more step for each level.

5
00:00:25.428 --> 00:00:26.507
It's right today.

6
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:32.494
Then someone at the bottom invites a friend, and the answer quietly leaves them out.

7
00:00:32.494 --> 00:00:33.118
No error.

8
00:00:33.118 --> 00:00:34.644
Just one person short.

9
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:35.988
Here's the data.

10
00:00:35.988 --> 00:00:40.295
The app's database is PostgreSQL, a free database program.

11
00:00:40.295 --> 00:00:50.393
Its customers table has 13 rows, and a column, referred_by, that holds the id of whoever invited each customer, or NULL when nobody did.

12
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.883
Draw those pointers and you get trees: a forest of five, with Asha at the top of one.

13
00:00:56.067 --> 00:01:00.652
Asha's chain is two levels deep today, so the level query asks two levels.

14
00:01:00.652 --> 00:01:06.043
The day Farid invites a friend, there's a third level, and the query never looks there.

15
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.265
How would you do it by hand?

16
00:01:08.265 --> 00:01:09.224
With a phone.

17
00:01:09.224 --> 00:01:11.511
Call Asha: whom did you invite?

18
00:01:11.511 --> 00:01:12.691
Chloe and Elena.

19
00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:15.100
Then call each person she named.

20
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.580
Chloe says Farid, Elena says Jonas.

21
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.209
Call those two: nobody.

22
00:01:19.209 --> 00:01:22.609
A round that names nobody new means you're done.

23
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:23.643
Notice the rule.

24
00:01:23.643 --> 00:01:28.124
In each round, you only call the people you heard about in the round before.

25
00:01:28.124 --> 00:01:30.955
You never needed to know how deep the chain was.

26
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.736
SQL can run that loop inside one query.

27
00:01:33.736 --> 00:01:39.412
WITH gives a query a name, here downline, and RECURSIVE lets that query read itself.

28
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:44.395
The whole thing is called a recursive common table expression, or CTE.

29
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:49.160
The first part, the anchor, is where the loop starts: Asha, at depth 0.

30
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:56.092
The second part, the recursive term, takes one step down: customers whose inviter is already in downline.

31
00:01:56.092 --> 00:01:59.117
UNION ALL stacks the two parts into one result.

32
00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:05.093
PostgreSQL runs it with a working table: the people you heard about in the last round of calls.

33
00:02:05.093 --> 00:02:06.320
Run the anchor once.

34
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.403
Then run the recursive term, reading only the working table.

35
00:02:10.403 --> 00:02:14.501
Its new rows join the result and become the next working table.

36
00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:16.973
Repeat until a round comes back empty.

37
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.948
So despite the name, nothing calls itself.

38
00:02:19.948 --> 00:02:22.524
The PostgreSQL docs call it iteration.

39
00:02:22.524 --> 00:02:26.592
Recursive is the name on the box; inside, it's a while loop.

40
00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:30.929
Now start from all five roots at once: everyone nobody invited.

41
00:02:30.929 --> 00:02:35.223
How many times does the recursive term run before the loop stops?

42
00:02:35.223 --> 00:02:36.280
Pause and guess.

43
00:02:39.533 --> 00:02:42.093
Round one finds 3, 4, 5 and 12.

44
00:02:42.093 --> 00:02:44.406
Round two finds 6, 8 and 10.

45
00:02:44.406 --> 00:02:46.800
Round three finds only Mateo.

46
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.168
Round four finds nobody, and the loop ends with all 13 customers.

47
00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:55.082
Four runs, and the last one finds nothing.

48
00:02:55.082 --> 00:02:57.093
Nobody wrote a stop condition.

49
00:02:57.093 --> 00:02:59.305
The data running out is the stop.

50
00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:06.151
On a copy with 50,000 customers, the whole forest takes 8 rounds and about 40 milliseconds.

51
00:03:06.333 --> 00:03:09.016
Two things follow from the working table.

52
00:03:09.016 --> 00:03:13.858
First, each round sees only the last round's rows, never the whole result.

53
00:03:13.858 --> 00:03:15.036
So it can't count.

54
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.864
Put count in the recursive term and PostgreSQL refuses.

55
00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:21.528
Count in the main query, after the loop.

56
00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:23.811
Second, there's no built-in limit.

57
00:03:23.811 --> 00:03:27.537
The loop stops when a round comes back empty, and only then.

58
00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:33.821
SQL Server, Microsoft's database program, stops after 100 levels by default.

59
00:03:33.821 --> 00:03:35.351
PostgreSQL doesn't.

60
00:03:35.533 --> 00:03:40.889
If the data ever loops back on itself, you'll be making calls until the battery dies.

61
00:03:40.889 --> 00:03:42.843
A later section adds the guard.

62
00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.434
So, Asha's thank-you.

63
00:03:44.434 --> 00:03:52.884
The loop has no depth written in it: start somewhere, take one step from the rows you found last, and stop when a step finds nothing.

64
00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:54.400
One quick check.

65
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.533
Start the walk from Ben instead.

66
00:03:56.533 --> 00:04:00.400
How many rows come back, and how many rounds find someone?

67
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.467
Pause and think.

68
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.545
Four rows: Ben, Daniel, Hugo and Mateo.

69
00:04:07.545 --> 00:04:11.412
Three rounds find someone, and a fourth finds nobody.
